VulkanLumen — Stage 3: GPU scene ray traversal + per-pass profiling

The public integration API is unchanged. All rendering and ray tracing remain
inside vulkan-systems/lumen/. vulkan-experiment continues to supply the window,
camera and Vulkan frame; it has no tracing shaders or geometry data.

Current rendering
  - Exact 16-face Cornell room from vulkan-experiment/path-tracer-bak.
  - Full-resolution raster primary visibility with depth testing.
  - HDR direct area-light shading using four deterministic samples.
  - Both rotated white blocks cast direct shadows.
  - Display composite/tonemap, no temporal reprojection or denoising.
  - No indirect GI or reflections yet.

Stage 3 scene tracing
  - Each non-emissive Cornell face is triangulated into two triangles:
    30 shadow-occluding triangles; 59 BVH nodes.
  - lumen/trace.cpp builds a balanced median-split static triangle BVH once
    on the CPU and uploads GPU buffers. Shader data uses the exact std430
    node/triangle layouts defined by VulkanBVH (bvh/bvh.hpp).
  - In the fragment shader, trace_scene(origin,direction,max_distance,any_hit)
    traverses the scene BVH with a bounded stack, exact two-sided
    Moller-Trumbore triangles and world-space ray distances.
  - The function returns closest-hit primitive, distance and barycentrics, or
    stops at the first hit for shadow queries. Unexpected stack overflow is
    marked so visibility does not silently treat it as a miss.
  - Lambertian lighting now uses this generic ray query for occlusion instead
    of Cornell-specific box bounds and analytic face intersections.
  - The shared VulkanBVH GPU builder / instanced TLAS traversal is NOT yet
    used: this baseline has one static 30-triangle demo. Its reusable data
    format makes upgrading the builder possible without changing host calls.
  - External scene handles are accepted by set_scene(), but loading arbitrary
    game meshes into this demo BVH is not implemented yet.

Frame timing
  - The Lumen API exposes gpu_performance(), with raster_ms, composite_ms,
    total_ms, supported and valid.
  - Query timestamps are used only if the selected graphics/compute queue
    supports them; readings come from the previous, fence-completed frame.
  - Vulkan-experiment prints every ~2 seconds:
      FPS; CPU frame avg and max ms; CPU input ms; CPU render/wait/submit ms;
      GPU scene (raster + inline ray queries) ms; composite ms; GPU total ms.
  - GPU time excludes CPU work and swapchain presentation. CPU render time
    includes any wait caused by frame scheduling or FIFO presentation.
  - No frame time or FPS claims are made until measured on the user's M1.

Run from vulkan-experiment/
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Expected: nearly the same image as Stage 2, with BVH-traced direct shadows,
rotated Cornell boxes, and periodic detailed timing logs. Camera: WASD/mouse.
Please send errors, screenshot and FPS/GPU timings before starting Stage 4.

Next: surface-card material/light capture and cached hit lighting.
Do not add a denoiser or full-scene path tracer to this harness.
